  "name": "Listening Riddles",
  "icon": "ear",
  "blurb": "Work out the answer from the clues you hear.",
  "childIntro": "Listen to the clues. They will not tell you the answer directly. You have to work it out.",
  "howItWorks": "The teacher reads a short scenario once and the child marks a picture. It is different from Following Directions because the answer is never stated. The child has to make a step of reasoning: the clues rule things out, and what is left is the answer. It appears only at OLSAT levels A to C.",
  "parentTip": "Play this at dinner. Give three clues about something in the room and let your child guess, then swap. The habit to build is crossing things off. After each clue, ask which options are now impossible. Children who guess from the first clue alone are the ones who get these wrong.",
